383","title":"sh_SKA3_1"},{"accession":"GSM3665382","title":"sh_NC_2"},{"accession":"GSM3665381","title":"sh_NC_1"},{"accession":"GSM3665384","title":"sh_SKA3_2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP188124","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP188/SRP188124/"}],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["33106477"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE128nnn/GSE128133/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA526524"},"200158933":{"uid":"200158933","accession":"GSE158933","gds":"","title":"Colonization of distant organs by mechanosensitive tumor cells via generation of stable circulating homotypic clusters","summary":"Mechanisms governing cancer metastasis remain poorly understood. Once disseminated tumor cells (DTCs) arrive at a metastatic organ, they remain there, latent, and become seeds of metastasis. However, the clonal composition of DTCs in a latent state remains unclear. Here, we applied high-resolution DNA barcode tracking to a mouse model that recapitulated the metastatic dormancy of head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC). We found that clones that had abundant circulating tumor cells (CTCs) but were rare in the primary site dominated DTCs. We obtained a specific subclone that dominated CTCs and subsequent DTCs in lungs and bone marrow. Despite no notable features under static conditions, this clone significantly generated stable cell aggregates that were resistant to anoikis in suspension culture under fluid shear stress conditions. Even as a heterogeneous population, this clone generated almost monoclonal CTC clusters. Via transcriptome analyses, we identified E-cadherin to be indispensable but not sufficient for such stable cell-cell adhesion. Instead, our data indicated that mutual regulation of cortical actin-myosin dynamics according to mechanostress in the cells within each aggregate governed stable adhesion. Furthermore, the gene expression signature of the mechanosensitive clone, which originated clustered CTCs, was associated with locoregional and metastatic recurrence in patients with HNSCC. These results reveal that intrinsically mechanosensitive rare clones in the parental population dominantly colonize distant sites via homotypic CTC cluster formation. This novel clonal selection mechanism based on intrinsic tumor cell mechanosensitivity may lead to promising cancer therapies.","gpl":"20795","gse":"158933","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2020/10/03","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4816032","title":"clone_EE_mRNA_seq"},{"accession":"GSM4816031","title":"clone_H_mRNA_seq"},{"accession":"GSM4816030","title":"Parental_mRNA_seq"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP286183","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP286/SRP286183/"}],"n_samples":3,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["33731803"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE158nnn/GSE158933/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA667054"},"200156178":{"uid":"200156178","accession":"GSE156178","gds":"","title":"Oncogenes overexpressed in metastatic oral cancers from patients with pain: potential pain mediators released in exosomes","summary":"Oral cancer patients experience pain at the site of the primary cancer.  Patients with metastatic oral cancers report greater pain.  Lack of pain identifies patients at low risk of metastasis with sensitivity = 0.94 and negative predictive value = 0.89.  In the same cohort, sensitivity and negative predictive value of depth of invasion, currently the best predictor, were 0.95 and 0.92, respectively.  Cancer pain is attributed to cancer-derived mediators that sensitize neurons and is associated with increased neuronal density.  We hypothesized that pain mediators would be overexpressed in metastatic cancers from patients reporting high pain.  We identified 40 genes overexpressed in metastatic cancers from patients reporting high pain (n=5) compared to N0 cancers (n=10) and normal tissue (n=5).  The genes are enriched for functions in extracellular matrix organization and angiogenesis.  They have oncogenic and neuronal functions and are reported in exosomes.  Hierarchical clustering according to expression of neurotrophic and axon guidance genes also separated cancers according to pain and nodal status.  Depletion of exosomes from cancer cell line supernatant reduced nociceptive behavior in a paw withdrawal assay, supporting a role for exosomes in cancer pain.  The identified genes and exosomes are potential therapeutic targets for stopping cancer and attenuating pain.","gpl":"16791","gse":"156178","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2020/09/07","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4726133","title":"gingiva5-A64044"},{"accession":"GSM4726136","title":"gingiva8-A64047"},{"accession":"GSM4726130","title":"gingiva2-A64040"},{"accession":"GSM4726150","title":"tongue9-A64034"},{"accession":"GSM4726139","title":"tcont2-A64035"},{"accession":"GSM4726135","title":"gingiva7-A64046"},{"accession":"GSM4726142","title":"tongue10-A64036"},{"accession":"GSM4726138","title":"tcont1-A64026"},{"accession":"GSM4726141","title":"tongue1-A64024"},{"accession":"GSM4726144","title":"tongue3-A64027"},{"accession":"GSM4726127","title":"gcont1-A64038"},{"accession":"GSM4726147","title":"tongue6-A64031"},{"accession":"GSM4726143","title":"tongue2-A64025"},{"accession":"GSM4726146","title":"tongue5-A64029"},{"accession":"GSM4726140","title":"tcont3-A64037"},{"accession":"GSM4726149","title":"ton