s of the Quaking (QKI) gene knock-down human oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) cells","summary":"Here, we elucidated whether miR-200 family members control RNA-binding protein quaking (QKI), a newly identified tumor suppressor that is regulated during EMT. QKI expression was suppressed by miR-200 overexpression, and the 3'-UTR of QKI mRNA was directly targeted by miR-200 in luciferase reporter assays. ShRNA-mediated knockdown of QKI led to pronounced EMT and protumor effects in both in vitro and in vivo studies of oral squamous cell carcinoma. Furthermore, high expression of QKI protein is associated with favorable prognosis in surgically resected head and neck squamous cell carcinoma and lung adenocarcinoma. In conclusion, QKI increases during EMT and is targeted by miR-200; while, it suppresses EMT and tumorigenesis. We suggest that QKI and miR-200 form a negative feedback loop to maintain homeostatic responses to EMT-inducing signals.","gpl":"16791","gse":"154730","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2020/07/21","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4677984","title":"shQKI2"},{"accession":"GSM4677981","title":"NTC2"},{"accession":"GSM4677983","title":"shQKI1"},{"accession":"GSM4677982","title":"NTC3"},{"accession":"GSM4677985","title":"shQKI3"},{"accession":"GSM4677980","title":"NTC1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP272585","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP272/SRP272585/"}],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE154nnn/GSE154730/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA647335"},"200153559":{"uid":"200153559","accession":"GSE153559","gds":"","title":"scRNAseq of activated cells of the B cell lineage in the tumor microenvironment","summary":"The goals of this study were to gain a better understanding of the heterogeneity and transcriptional profile of activated cells of the B cell lineage in the tumor microenvironment.","gpl":"21290","gse":"153559","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2020/07/20","suppfile":"MTX, TSV, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4647118","title":"activated CD19+ cells TIL pt1"},{"accession":"GSM4647120","title":"activated CD19+ cells TIL pt2"},{"accession":"GSM4647123","title":"activated CD19+ cells metLN pt3"},{"accession":"GSM4647122","title":"activated CD19+ cells TIL pt3"},{"accession":"GSM4647119","title":"activated CD19+ cells metLN pt1"},{"accession":"GSM4647124","title":"activated CD19+ cells Flu PBMC"},{"accession":"GSM4647121","title":"activated CD19+ cells metLN pt2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP269383","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP269/SRP269383/"}],"n_samples":7,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["33208941"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE153nnn/GSE153559/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA643236"},"200149327":{"uid":"200149327","accession":"GSE149327","gds":"","title":"RNA-sequencing analysis of tumor-infiltrating B cells","summary":"Tumour-infiltrating lymphocytes are a major predictor for overall survival and response to immunotherapy for several malignancies. However, while tumour-infiltrating CD8 T cells have been extensively studied, little is known about the role of the humoral immune system within the tumour microenvironment (TME). Here we show that plasma cells are present in the TME and actively secrete human papillomavirus (HPV)-specific IgG antibodies in HPV-positive head and neck squamous cell carcinoma patients. Compared to circulating memory B cells, plasma cells in the TME were enriched for HPV-reactivity with little bystander recruitment of influenza-specific cells. HPV-specific plasma cells in the TME correlated with the antibody titres in peripheral blood and produced antibodies with a high degree of somatic hypermutation directed against HPV E2, E6 and E7 antigens. A striking proportion of HPV-specific antibodies produced in situ were directed against E2, which might thus be a promising target for immunotherapies in HPV-associated cancers. Overall, we show that antigen-specific activated and germinal centre B cells as well as plasma cells can be found in the TME, with plasma cells producing antibodies specific for tumour-associated antigens in situ. These findings provide a better understanding of humoral immune responses in human cancer and suggest that tumour-infiltrating B cells could be harnessed for the development of novel therapeutic agents.","gpl":"21290","gse":"149327","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2020/07/20","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4497816","title":"Metastatic lymph node activated B cells [HPV4LN_ABCneg]"},{"accession":"GSM4497819","title":"Tumor infiltrating activated B cells [HPV29TIL_ABCneg]"},{"accession":"GSM4497813","title":"PBMC activated B cells (7 days post flu-vaccine) [EVC379_ABC]"},{"accession":"GSM4497827","title":"Metastatic lymph node naive B cells [HPV22LN_naive]"},{"accession":"GSM4497814","title":"PBMC antibody secreting cells (7 days post flu-vaccine) [EVC379_ASC]"},{"accession":"GSM4497825","title":"Metastatic lymph node activated B cells [HPV22LN_ABCneg]"},{"accession":"GSM4497822","title":"Tumor infiltrating activated B cells [HPV40TIL_ABCneg]"},{"accession":"GSM4497826","title":"Metastatic lymph node antibody secreting cells [HPV22LN_ASC]"},{"accession":"GSM4497823","title":"