alysis in cancer patients could provide clues indicating tumor sensitivity to immune checkpoint blockade (ICB). Data suggests a role for costimulatory pathways, particularly CD28, in exhausted T-cell responsiveness to PD-1/PD-L1 blockade. Here, we used single-cell transcriptomic, phenotypic, and functional approaches to dissect the relation between CD8+ T-cell exhaustion, CD28 costimulation, and tumor specificity in head and neck, cervical, and ovarian cancers. We found that memory tumor-specific CD8+ T cells, but not bystander cells, sequentially express immune checkpoints once they infiltrate tumors leading, in situ, to a functionally exhausted population. Exhausted T cells were nonetheless endowed with effector and tumor residency potential but exhibited loss of the costimulatory receptor CD28 in comparison to their circulating memory counterparts. Accordingly, PD-1 inhibition improved proliferation of circulating tumor-specific CD8 T cells and reversed functional exhaustion of specific T cells at tumor sites. In agreement with their tumor specificity, high infiltration of tumors by exhausted cells was predictive of response to therapy and survival in ICB-treated head and neck cancer patients. Our results showed that PD-1 blockade–mediated proliferation/reinvigoration of circulating memory T cells and local reversion of exhaustion occur concurrently to control tumors.","gpl":"21697","gse":"148162","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2020/05/01","suppfile":"TSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4455312","title":"S526_CD8"},{"accession":"GSM4455315","title":"S626_CD8"},{"accession":"GSM4455313","title":"S533_CD8"},{"accession":"GSM4455314","title":"S624_CD8"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP255417","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP255/SRP255417/"}],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["32295784","33332284"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE148nnn/GSE148162/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA623361"},"200146483":{"uid":"200146483","accession":"GSE146483","gds":"","title":"Development of gene expression signatures for oral cancer","summary":"To further development of gene expression approach to squamous carcinoma, we have employed whole genome microarray expression profiling as a discovery platform to identify genes with the potential to reveal the relation between oral squamous carcinoma (OSCC) and human normal oral mucosal epithelial keratinocytes.","gpl":"17077","gse":"146483","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2020/03/06","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4387011","title":"Oral squamous carcinoma (OSCC) [HSC4 (1)]"},{"accession":"GSM4387019","title":"Human normal oral mucosal epithelial keratinocytes [Kera (1)]"},{"accession":"GSM4387014","title":"Oral squamous carcinoma (OSCC) [HSC3 (2)]"},{"accession":"GSM4387017","title":"Human normal oral mucosal epithelial keratinocytes [Kera (I)]"},{"accession":"GSM4387018","title":"Human normal oral mucosal epithelial keratinocytes [Kera (S)]"},{"accession":"GSM4387012","title":"Oral squamous carcinoma (OSCC) [Sa3 new]"},{"accession":"GSM4387015","title":"Oral squamous carcinoma (OSCC) [HSC4 (2)]"},{"accession":"GSM4387009","title":"Oral squamous carcinoma (OSCC) [HSC2 (1)]"},{"accession":"GSM4387013","title":"Oral squamous carcinoma (OSCC) [HSC2 (2)]"},{"accession":"GSM4387016","title":"Oral squamous carcinoma (OSCC) [Sa3 (2)]"},{"accession":"GSM4387010","title":"Oral squamous carcinoma (OSCC) [HSC3 (1)]"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":11,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["33723306"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE146nnn/GSE146483/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA610649"},"200145599":{"uid":"200145599","accession":"GSE145599","gds":"","title":"The function and mechanism about how ORAOV1-B potentiates the OSCC metastasis.","summary":"Lymph node metastasis, a powerful prognostic indicator of oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC), is chiefly responsible for cancer death. Long non-coding RNA (lncRNA) is recently addressed to significantly account for modulating OSCC metastasis. Here, we identified a novel alternative splice of Oral Cancer Overexpressed 1 (ORAOV1), ORAOV1-B, which was subsequently validated as an lncRNA and correlated with OSCC lymph node metastasis; significantly increased invasion and migration were observed in ORAOV1-B-overexpressed OSCC cells; In the metastatic model, ORAOV1-B also significantly contributed to OSCC-related lung metastasis. cDNA microarrays was performed to compare up- or down-regulated genes in EV and ORAOV1-B sets of OSCC, which suggested TNFα as a potential downstream of ORAOV1-B and the upregulation of pro-inflammatory genes, indicating the activation of NF-κB pathway. In summary, the novel splice variant ORAOV1-B is an lncRNA, which significantly potentiates OSCC invasion and metastasis by binding to Hsp90 and activating the NF-κB-TNFα loop. Our study implies that ORAOV1-B might serve as an attractive OSCC metastasis intervention target.","gpl":"17077","gse":"145599","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2020/03/04","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4322070","title":"HSC-4 ORAOV1-B overexpression"},{"accession":"GSM4322068","title":"HSC-3 ORAOV1-B overexpression"},{"accession":"GSM4322067","title":"HSC-3 EV"},{"accession":"GSM4322069","title":"HSC-4 EV"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","s