le":"PDIS9061-252"},{"accession":"GSM3568100","title":"PDIS9061-347"},{"accession":"GSM3568303","title":"PDIS9061-585"},{"accession":"GSM3568306","title":"PDIS9061-590"},{"accession":"GSM3568094","title":"PDIS9061-341"},{"accession":"GSM3568177","title":"PDIS9061-437"},{"accession":"GSM3568203","title":"PDIS9061-467"},{"accession":"GSM3567991","title":"PDIS9061-218"},{"accession":"GSM3568197","title":"PDIS9061-460"},{"accession":"GSM3567994","title":"PDIS9061-222"},{"accession":"GSM3567911","title":"PDIS9061-131"},{"accession":"GSM3568409","title":"PDIS9061-705"},{"accession":"GSM3567891","title":"PDIS9061-106"},{"accession":"GSM3567808","title":"PDIS9061-011"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP180022","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP180/SRP180022/"}],"n_samples":669,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["34632380"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE125nnn/GSE125288/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA515750"},"200126191":{"uid":"200126191","accession":"GSE126191","gds":"","title":"Dietary-phytochemical mediated reversion of cancer-specific splicing inhibits Warburg effect in head and neck cancer","summary":"It’s a well-established fact that epigenetic changes regulate alternative splicing in a context-dependent manner, as well as the epigenetic modifying effect of curcumin, is known in cancer. In this study, we have investigated the role of curcumin in the regulation of alternative splicing through epigenetic alterations. We performed Human Transcriptome Array 2.0 to understand the global changes in gene expression as well as alternative splicing upon curcumin-PEC treatment.","gpl":"17585","gse":"126191","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2019/08/31","suppfile":"CEL, CHP","samples":[{"accession":"GSM3593138","title":"Curcumin-PEC biological replicate_1"},{"accession":"GSM3593137","title":"Control-PEC biological replicate_2"},{"accession":"GSM3593139","title":"Curcumin-PEC biological replicate_2"},{"accession":"GSM3593136","title":"Control-PEC biological replicate_1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["31675998"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE126nnn/GSE126191/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA521258"},"200118785":{"uid":"200118785","accession":"GSE118785","gds":"","title":"H3K36me2 recruits DNMT3A and shapes intergenic DNA methylation landscapes","summary":"Enzymes catalyzing CpG methylation in DNA, including DNMT1 and DNMT3A/B, are indispensable for mammalian tissue development and homeostasis. They are also implicated in human developmental disorders and cancers, supporting a critical role of DNA methylation during cell fate specification and maintenance. Recent studies suggest that histone post-translational modifications (PTMs) are involved in specifying patterns of DNMT localization and DNA methylation at promoters and actively transcribed gene bodies. However, mechanisms governing the establishment and maintenance of intergenic DNA methylation remain poorly understood. Germline mutations in DNMT3A lead to a childhood overgrowth syndrome that is phenotypically overlapping with Sotos syndrome caused by haploinsufficiency of NSD1, a histone methyltransferase catalyzing di-methylation on H3K36 (H3K36me2), pointing to a potential mechanistic link between the two disorders. Here we report that NSD1-mediated H3K36me2 is required for recruitment of DNMT3A and maintenance of DNA methylation at intergenic regions. Genome-wide analysis shows binding and activity of DNMT3A are co-localized with H3K36me2 at non-coding regions of euchromatin. Genetic ablation of NSD1 and its paralogue NSD2 in mouse and human cells redistributes DNMT3A to H3K36me3-marked gene bodies and reduces intergenic DNA methylation. NSD1 mutant tumors and Sotos patient samples are also associated with intergenic DNA hypomethylation. Consistently, PWWP-domain of DNMT3A shows dual recognition of H3K36me2/3 in vitro with a higher binding affinity towards H3K36me2, which is abrogated by overgrowth syndrome-derived missense mutations. Taken together, our study uncovers a trans-chromatin regulatory pathway that, when perturbed, promotes neoplastic and developmental overgrowth.","gpl":"25476;21103;17021;20795;21227;24247;21273;25475","gse":"118785","taxon":"Drosophila melanogaster; Homo sapiens; Mus musculus","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Genome binding/occupancy profiling by high throughput sequencing; Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing; Methylation profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2019/08/30","suppfile":"BW, TDF","samples":[{"accession":"GSM3347555","title":"HA-DNMT3A_ChIPseq_NSD1_2_DKO"},{"accession":"GSM3347558","title":"H3K36me2_ChIPseq_Fadu"},{"accession":"GSM3772692","title":"HA-DNMT3A2_ChIPseq_mESC_DNMT3A_KO"},{"accession":"GSM3347552","title":"HA-DNMT3A_I310N_ChIPseq_Parental"},{"accession":"GSM3772686","title":"H3K36me3_ChIPseq_TKO"},{"accession":"GSM3347566","title":"WGBS_Cal27"},{"accession":"GSM3347546","title":"H3K36me2_ChIPseq_NSD1_2_DKO"},{"accession":"GSM3772689","title":"H3K36me3_ChIPseq_mESC_Parental"},{"accession":"GSM3347569","title":"WGBS_SKN_3"},{"accession":"GSM3347549","title":"H3K36me2_ChIPseq_mESCs"},{"accession":"GSM3347553","title":"HA-DNMT3A_ChIPseq_SETD2_KO"},{"accession":"GSM3772693","title":"HA-DNMT3A2_ChIPseq_mESC_NSD1_KO"},{"accession":"GSM3347550","title":"HA-DNMT3A_ChIPseq_Parental"},{"accession":"GSM3772690","title":"H3K36me3_ChIPseq_mESC_NSD1_KO"},{"accession":"GSM3347544","title":"H3K27ac_ChIPseq_Parental"},{"accession":"GSM3347564","title":"WGBS_Parental"},{"accession":"GSM3347547","title":"H