ary":"The majority of cancer patients do not respond to immunotherapy. In order to systematically discover pathways promoting cancer cell resistance to effector immune cells, we generated immunity-resistant Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma cell lines. We utilized RNA-Seq to determine what are the genes and pathways that are significantly altered when cancer cells become resistant to effectors.","gpl":"11154","gse":"100828","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2018/12/25","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM2694111","title":"50133: Immune-resistant PCI-13"},{"accession":"GSM2694112","title":"50134: Immune-resistant PCI-13"},{"accession":"GSM2694109","title":"50131: PCI-13 Wildtype"},{"accession":"GSM2694110","title":"50132: PCI-13 Wildtype"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP111135","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP111/SRP111135/"}],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["29769207"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE100nnn/GSE100828/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA393191"},"200107403":{"uid":"200107403","accession":"GSE107403","gds":"","title":"Knockdown of DKK3 decreases malignant property in  head and neck squamous cell carcinoma-derived HSC-3 cells.","summary":"We hypothesized that DKK3 may exert oncogenic function supecifically in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC). DKK3 knockdown in HNSCC cell resulted in decreased cellular proliferation, migration, invasion and in vivo tumor growth.","gpl":"13915","gse":"107403","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2018/11/30","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM2866365","title":"HSC3-shDKK3"},{"accession":"GSM2866363","title":"HSC3 parental cell"},{"accession":"GSM2866364","title":"HSC3-shScr"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":3,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE107nnn/GSE107403/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA420015"},"200122512":{"uid":"200122512","accession":"GSE122512","gds":"","title":"Gene expression analysis of head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) cell lines.","summary":"Human cancer cell lines are the most frequently used preclinical models in the study of cancer biology and the development of therapeutics. Although anatomically diverse, human papillomavirus (HPV)-driven cancers have a common etiology and similar mutations that overlap with but are distinct from those found in HPV-negative cancers. Building on prior studies that have characterized subsets of head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) and cervical squamous cell carcinoma (CESC) cell lines separately, we performed genomic, viral gene expression, and viral integration analyses on 74 cell lines that include all readily-available HPV-positive (9 HNSCC, 8 CESC) and CESC (8 HPV-positive, 2 HPV-negative) cell lines and 55 HPV-negative HNSCC cell lines. We used over 700 human tumors for comparison. Mutation patterns in the cell lines were similar to those of human tumors. We confirmed HPV viral protein and mRNA expression in the HPV-positive cell lines. We found HPV types in three CESC cell lines that are distinct from those previously reported. We found that cell lines and tumors had similar patterns of viral gene expression; there were few sites of recurrent HPV integration. As seen in tumors, HPV integration did appear to alter host gene expression in cell lines. The HPV-positive cell lines had higher levels of p16 and lower levels of Rb protein expression than did the HPV-negative lines. Although the number of HPV-positive cell lines is limited, our results suggest that these cell lines represent suitable models for studying HNSCC and CESC, both of which are common and lethal.","gpl":"21290","gse":"122512","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2018/11/15","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM3473122","title":"UMSCC104"},{"accession":"GSM3473119","title":"UPCISCC152"},{"accession":"GSM3473102","title":"UMSCC11A"},{"accession":"GSM3473056","title":"UMSCC6"},{"accession":"GSM3473099","title":"SCC25"},{"accession":"GSM3473062","title":"UMSCC33"},{"accession":"GSM3473082","title":"UDSCC2"},{"accession":"GSM3473079","title":"HN30"},{"accession":"GSM3473059","title":"HN5"},{"accession":"GSM3473085","title":"X183"},{"accession":"GSM3473105","title":"UMSCC22A"},{"accession":"GSM3473068","title":"SCC4"},{"accession":"GSM3473111","title":"CASKI"},{"accession":"GSM3473108","title":"UMSCC47"},{"accession":"GSM3473065","title":"PCI15B"},{"accession":"GSM3473091","title":"C33A"},{"accession":"GSM3473067","title":"SCC15"},{"accession":"GSM3473088","title":"UMSCC22B"},{"accession":"GSM3473064","title":"OSC19"},{"accession":"GSM3473084","title":"UMSCC17A"},{"accession":"GSM3473104","title":"UMSCC14A"},{"accession":"GSM3473087","title":"UMSCC17B"},{"accession":"GSM3473061","title":"PCI13"},{"accession":"GSM3473113","title":"HELA"},{"accession":"GSM3473076","title":"MDA686LN"},{"accession":"GSM3473073","title":"PE.CAPJ34"},{"accession":"GSM3473093","title":"HMS001"},{"accession":"GSM3473090","title":"UTSCC45"},{"accession":"GSM3473053","title":"UMSCC10A"},{"accession":"GSM3473107","title":"UMSCC4"},{"accession":"GSM3473050","title":"Ca922"},{"accession":"GSM3473096","title":"ME180"},{"accession":"GSM3473070","title":"TR146"},{"accession":"GSM3473116","title":"MDA1986LN"},{"accession":"GSM3473066","title":"PCI24"},{"accession":"GSM3473110","title":"X1483"},{"accession":"GSM3473092","title":"C4.II"},{"accession":"GSM3473112","title":"HT3"},{"accession":"GSM3473089","title":"UPCISCC154"},{"accession":"GSM3473069","title":"SCC9"},{"accession":"GSM3473109","title":"UPCISCC090"},{"accession":"GSM3473115","title":"MDA1186"},{"accession":"GSM3473052","title":"UMSCC1"},{"accession":"GSM3473058","title":"HN4"},{"accession":"GSM3473095","title":"MDA1586"},{"accession":"GSM3473055","title":"UMSCC25"},{"accession":"GSM3473121","title":"MDA1686"},{"accession":"GSM3473118","title":"UMSCC14B"},{"accession":"GSM3473098","title":"PCI15A"},{"accession":"GSM3473077","title":"SCC61"},{"accession":"GSM3473057","title":"CAL27"},{"accession":"GSM3473114","title":"JHU011"},{"accession":"GSM3473072","title":"MDA886LN"},{"accession":"GSM3473074","title":"X584A2"},{"accession":"GSM3473071","title":"C4.I"},{"accession":"GSM3473054","title":"UMSCC19"},{"accession":"GSM3473097","title":"MS751"},{"accession":"GSM3473051","title":"FaDu"},{"accession":"GSM3473101","title":"SW756"},{"accession":"GSM3473103","title":"UMSCC11B"},{"accession":"GSM3473075","title":"MDA1386TU"},{"accession":"GSM3473086","title":"DETROIT562"},{"accession":"GSM3473083","title":"UMSCC10B"},{"accession":"GSM3473081","title":"JHU022"},{"accession":"GSM3473080","title":"HN31"},{"accession":"GSM3473078","title":"SIHA"},{"accession":"GSM3473100","title":"SqCC.Y1"},{"accession":"GSM3473063","title":"UMSCC85"},{"accession":"GSM3473106","title":"UMSCC3"},{"accession":"GSM3473060","title":"MSK922"},{"accession":"GSM3473094","title":"MDA1386LN"},{"accession":"GSM3473120","title":"JHU029"},{"accession":"GSM3473117","title":"MDA686TU"},{"accession":"GSM3473123","title":"VU147T"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":74,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["30626753","33108350"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE122nnn/GSE122512/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA505441"},"200110045":{"uid":"200110045","accession":"GSE110045","gds":"","title":"Generic drug screen identifies paracetamol as 5-aza-2’-deoxycytidine sensitizer in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma cells","summary":"Aberrant DNA methylation (5mC) is one of the key characteristics of many cancers including head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC). The DNA demethylating agent 5-aza-2’-deoxycytidine (DAC) has anti-cancer therapeutic potential, but its clinical efficacy is currently hindered by dose-limiting side effects. Here we investigated the potential use of DAC in the treatment of HNSCC and show that its efficacy is primarily dependent on the ability of DAC to demethylate DNA. In order to establish whether HNSCC cells can be sensitized to DAC, a panel of 100 generic drugs were screened in combination with DAC. While the 100-drug panel did not sensitise DAC-resistant HNSCC cell lines to DAC treatment, the screen identified that paracetamol (acetaminophen), valproic acid and zinc acetate significantly enhanced DAC efficacy in the DAC-responsive cell lines. DAC and paracetamol were established to work in synergy, allowing DAC to be used at therapeutically relevant low doses (below 500nM). The mechanisms underlying the DAC-paracetamol synergy are multifactorial and encompass both effects of DAC on paracetamol action (alterations in the cyclooxygenase (COX) pathway and mimicry of paracetamol overdose) as well as decreased DNA methylation by paracetamol.   Therefore, we propose DAC to be a potential therapeutic in a subset of HNSCC patients with its efficacy significantly increased by use of the common analgesic paracetamol. The DAC-paracetamol synergy should also be considered in cancers with an approved DAC treatment regime.","gpl":"18573","gse":"110045","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2018/11/02","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM2976977","title":"VU40T_DAC_Para"},{"accession":"GSM2976976","title":"VU40T_Para"},{"accession":"GSM2976974","title":"VU40T_Control"},{"accession":"GSM2976975","title":"VU40T_DAC"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP132039","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP132/SRP132039/"}],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["33359448"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE110nnn/GSE110045/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA432654"},"200121848":{"uid":"200121848","accession":"GSE121848","gds":"","title":"miRNA profiles of exosomes secreted by head and neck cancer-associated fibroblasts and the paired normal fibroblasts","summary":"Exosomal miRNAs secreted by cancer-associated fibroblasts (CAFs) play a critical role in facilitating head and neck cancer (HNC) progression. A few studies in other cancers have confirmed some CAF-specific miRNAs could be delivered to tumor cells via exosome, thus contributing to chemoresistance. To identify the specific miRNAs responsible for the capability of CAF-derived exosomes to promote cisplatin resistance in recipient HNC cells, a miRNA array was conducted to identify miRNAs involved in exosomes derived from CAFs and the paired normal fibroblasts (NFs).","gpl":"16384","gse":"121848","taxon":"Homo sapiens; synthetic construct","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2018/10/27","suppfile":"CEL","samples":[{"accession":"GSM3447733","title":"NF-Exos: NF-derived exosomes"},{"accession":"GSM3447734","title":"CAF-Exos: CAF-derived exosomes"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":2,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["30642385"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE121nnn/GSE121848/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA498628"},"200092845":{"uid":"200092845","accession":"GSE92845","gds":"","title":"A longitudinal model of human neuronal differentiation for functional studies of schizophrenia polygenic risk","summary":"Human neural stem cells were differentiation to a neuronal lineage over 30 days. Genome-wide gene expression was collected at seven time points in at least triplicates. We used these expression profiles to identify longitunal gene clusters that are expressed over time and map GWAS disease risk onto this. We show that schizophrenia disease is enriched in specific clusters across in vitro neuronal differentiation, which could make this an applicable model to study this disease.","gpl":"10558","gse":"92845","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2018/10/05","suppfile":"IDAT, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM2438457","title":"hNSC-Day2-rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2438454","title":"hNSC-Day5-rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2438446","title":"hNSC-Day0-rep4"},{"accession":"GSM2438443","title":"hNSC-Day2-rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2438460","title":"hNSC-Day2-rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2438440","title":"hNSC-Day30-rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2438439","title":"hNSC-Day10-rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2438462","title":"hNSC-Day5-rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2438459","title":"hNSC-Day30-rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2438448","title":"hNSC-Day0-rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2438442","title":"hNSC-Day20-rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2438451","title":"hNSC-Day30-rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2438447","title":"hNSC-Day10-rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2438444","title":"hNSC-Day15-rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2438445","title":"hNSC-Day2-rep4"},{"accession":"GSM2438456","title":"hNSC-Day0-rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2438453","title":"hNSC-Day0-rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2438450","title":"hNSC-Day5-rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2438449","title":"hNSC-Day30-rep4"},{"accession":"GSM2438461","title":"hNSC-Day15-rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2438441","title":"hNSC-Day15-rep3"},{"accessi